Draw the application from the model, and put it under a camera

The prototype's twelve hardcoded collections are gone. Every figure on the page
now arrives from /api/state: the fifteen, the market's 577 candidates, the
fixtures and their difficulty, the gameweek rail and its deadlines, the standing
overrides and the benchmarks a transfer is measured against.

The script moves out of the document into /assets/app.js. That is not tidying: a
Content-Security-Policy that has to allow 'unsafe-inline' to run an inline script
also allows whatever an injected string manages to open, so out-of-line is the
precondition for a policy worth having.

Three second implementations were found and removed, all of them disagreeing with
the model they duplicated:

  - role(mins) decided the role band from thresholds of 80/65/40 over four names.
    analysis.rotationLabel uses 75/60/40/20 over five. A player on 78 expected
    minutes read "Likely starter" on the page and counted as "nailed" in the
    squad that had just been picked around him. The band now arrives decided; this
    file chooses a colour for it and nothing else. The fifth band needed a fifth
    dot in the stylesheet.
  - The overrides panel computed staleness as daysSince(chk) > 14. The rule in
    force is seven days, or within two gameweeks of lapsing -- a second condition
    that arithmetic cannot express at all. So an override the model considered
    overdue rendered as fine for another week.
  - The same panel re-formatted dates the server had already phrased, producing
    "lapses after indefinite — review" and "checked 2026-07-10 (40d) (40d)".

And the escaping hole is closed. There were 24 innerHTML assignments and no escape
helper anywhere; the only textContent uses were numbers. Safe with mock data, and
a stored-XSS channel the moment real player names and FPL's own news prose flow
through it. The Go template being replaced escaped everything for free.

Two figures in the markup were literals that made the page contradict itself: the
same fifteen was worth £99.5m on the pitch and £100.0m on the Players tab, and the
"weakest starter" legend named four players from the prototype's squad rather than
this one. The overrides badge was frozen at 9 with no id to write to.

The storage note now says what is true. It promised "Saved on this device as you
go" while the prototype had no localStorage, no sessionStorage and no cookie at
all, so a reload discarded everything. Writing is stage two; until then the page
does not claim otherwise.

The visual regression is the point of the rest. Nine screenshots at 1440 and 390,
against the committed GW1 capture with the clock and the token pinned, compared
pixel-for-pixel with no tolerance -- the inputs cannot move, so neither may the
output. The differ is stdlib image/png and writes both what it got and a diff with
the changes lit up, because a failure should be something you look at.

It earned itself immediately: the first run produced nine screenshots of the
landing page, because every shot named a panel and no path. Both the £100.0m
contradiction and the doubled "(40d)" were found by looking at what it produced.

Chromium here is a snap, which gets a PRIVATE /tmp: it reports success, writes the
screenshot into its own namespace, and the host sees nothing. t.TempDir() is
therefore unusable and the scratch lives under the package.

capture.Replay's fixture is registered in internal/backtest's unwiredBaseline
rather than exempted quietly. It builds an engine with no recency index, and the
guard against that is a good one -- but at GW1 GameweeksPlayed() is 0 and
cmd/armband/main.go gates both recency and priors above zero, so the live binary
builds the same unwired engine. Attaching one would make the fixture less faithful,
not more. A test asserts that premise, so repointing the capture at a mid-season
week fails loudly instead of leaving the exemption wrong in the flattering
direction.
